eed to bring them in.\n\nGiven the concerns about B's interpersonal issues, perhaps we should consider other candidates. Let's evaluate quickly:\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: unfriendly, eats unhealthily. No info on stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention accuracy, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability. Leadership suggests some crew cooperation, but unfriendly suggests poor communication. Not enough info.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (crew cooperation), not very willing to further his education (adaptability), puts concern for others above everything (maybe good crew cooperation), excellent attention skills (attention accuracy). So mixed: good decision making, attention, but communication and egocentric issues.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), considered moody (crew cooperation), regarded as a loner (crew cooperation). So strong technical and responsibility, but moodiness and loner may affect crew cooperation.\n\nWe need to compare B's strengths vs concerns. B has strong reliability (conscientious), stress handling, weather assessment, computer skills. But concerns: grumpy, uncooperative, memory for numbers (attention/accuracy), nasty remarks (communication). Memory for numbers is important for navigation.\n\nCandidate D has strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, responds to unexpected events. Moodiness and loner are concerns for crew cooperation. No mention of memory for numbers, but maybe okay.\n\nCandidate C has quick correct decisions, excellent attention, but communication issues and egocentric.\n\nCandidate A has anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but unfriendly and unhealthy.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate overall best fits. B's interpersonal issues seem serious. D's moodiness also serious but maybe less than B's uncooperative? D's strengths are strong across many technical criteria. D's concerns are crew cooperation. No mention of memory for numbers.\n\nThus D might be a strong alternative. We could propose D as provisional recommendation, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns, and ask for any additional evidence on D's crew cooperation or mood impact.\n\nWe should also ask other panel members for any observations on D's crew cooperation and professional communication.\n\nThus our contribution: shift recommendation to D, explain why B's concerns outweigh his strengths relative to D's strengths and concerns. Use explicit facts.\n\nLet's craft: \"Given B's grumpy/uncooperative and memory issues, I am less convinced. Candidate D shows strong technical competence (problem solving, concentration, responsibility) and handles unexpected events, which aligns with stress resilience and decision quality. While D is moody and a loner, we need to assess impact on crew cooperation. I propose D as provisional recommendation, pending any further evidence on his teamwork.\"\n\nWe need to include vote in metadata: Candidate D.\n\nProceed."}
